  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1223 PM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nA trough and accompanying front is forecast to move across South-\nCentral Texas on Thursday. The moisture supply for this front will \nbe limited since only two days will have passed since the previous \nfront, so low rain chances (20-40%) are limited to the Coastal \nPlains. The passage of that front will clear the air again for a dry \nand seasonable Friday. \n\nOver the weekend, a southern branch polar trough is set to amplify \nacross the Central Plains and the Midwest. This sets up a favorable \npattern for northerly flow aloft, which should reinforce cool air \nover Texas. It does not look like this will bring a sharp change in \ntemperatures, but highs over the weekend look to gradually decline \ninto the 60s and low 70s with overnight lows in the 40s becoming \nmore widespread. There is a 20 percent chance of rain this weekend \nfor areas south of I-10 as this oozing of cool air interacts with \nmoisture delivered by a subtropical trough over Mexico. If the \nsubtropical trough is further away, as depicted in the GFS/GEFS and \ntheir ML counterparts, then this rain will tend to be lighter. If \nthe trough is closer, as depicted in the ECMWF/EPS and their ML \ncounterparts, then there may be more meaningful amounts above 1\". \nThere is ample uncertainty regarding the timing of that trough, so \nrain chances this weekend may vary significantly in subsequent \nforecasts until the trough becomes better defined. In any case, the \nweekend should be slightly cooler than average for mid-November. \nConditions early next week will also depend on whether the trough \nclears out of our area, but the overall wavy upper-air pattern over \nthe western US should set up additional opportunities for cooler and \ndrier air to cycle into the area.",
